title: "Referential Common Ground paper repository"
version: "v0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-10-25"

authors:
  - family-names: Case
    given-names: Denise M.
    orcid: https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6165-7389
    affiliation: "Northwest Missouri State University, School of Computer Science and Information Systems, Maryville, MO, USA"

repository-code: "https://github.com/structural-explainability/paper-110-common-ground"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"

abstract: >-
  This repository contains the paper Referential Common Ground:
  Conditions for Compatibility Between a Bounded Referential Fragment
  and Interpretive Frameworks. The paper investigates the Referential
  Common Ground assumption underlying Neutral Substrate theory.
  Using classical propositional logic, it establishes that
  admissibility alone does not guarantee nontrivial common ground.
  It characterizes frameworks neutral on a vocabulary through
  their permitted valuations and proves their compatibility
  with every satisfiable theory over that vocabulary.
  The results are formalized in Lean 4 and are relative to
  explicitly bounded fragments and framework classes.
  They do not establish global Referential Common Ground
  or independently justify a fragment's referential commitments.

keywords:
  - formal ontology
  - neutral substrates
  - referential common ground
  - referential fragments
  - interpretive frameworks
  - framework admissibility
  - framework compatibility
  - propositional logic
  - joint consistency
  - vocabulary neutrality
  - machine-checked proofs
  - persistent interpretive disagreement

